-- title:
--   A positive Leopoldt defect of a CM field descends to its maximal real subfield
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be an odd prime and let $\mathbb{K}$ be a CM number field, that is, a totally complex quadratic extension of its maximal real subfield
--
--   $$\mathbb{K}^{+} \;=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{K} \;:\; \overline{\varphi(x)} = \varphi(x) \text{ for every } \varphi : \mathbb{K} \to \mathbb{C} \,\}.$$
--
--   If the Leopoldt defect of $\mathbb{K}$ at $p$ is positive, then so is the Leopoldt defect of $\mathbb{K}^{+}$ at $p$:
--
--   $$\mathcal{D}_L(\mathbb{K}) > 0 \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \mathcal{D}_L(\mathbb{K}^{+}) > 0 .$$
--
--   Equivalently, in contrapositive form: Leopoldt's conjecture for the totally real field $\mathbb{K}^{+}$ implies it for $\mathbb{K}$. This is the step by which the source turns its Theorem 1, a statement about CM fields, into the assumption it actually argues from - that $\mathbb{K}^{+}$ carries a non-cyclotomic $\mathbb{Z}_p$-extension. Via Iwasawa's isomorphism $\mathrm{Gal}(\Omega(\mathbb{K})/\mathbb{K}) \cong \mathbb{Z}_p^{\,r_2+1+\mathcal{D}_L(\mathbb{K})}$ a positive defect is exactly an excess of $\mathbb{Z}_p$-extensions, and the excess for $\mathbb{K}$ is read off on $\mathbb{K}^{+}$.
--
--   The reason the implication holds is that passing from $\mathbb{K}^{+}$ to $\mathbb{K}$ cannot raise Dirichlet's rank while it can only raise the $p$-adic one. Indeed $\mathbb{Z}\text{-rk}\,E(\mathbb{K}^{+}) = \mathbb{Z}\text{-rk}\,E(\mathbb{K})$, because $\mathbb{K}$ and $\mathbb{K}^{+}$ have the same number of infinite places (Hasse: the subgroup generated by the real units and the roots of unity has index $1$ or $2$ in $E(\mathbb{K})$); and the semilocal units of $\mathbb{K}^{+}$ at $p$ embed continuously into those of $\mathbb{K}$, compatibly with the diagonal embeddings of the global units, so $\mathbb{Z}_p\text{-rk}\,\bar{E}(\mathbb{K}^{+}) \le \mathbb{Z}_p\text{-rk}\,\bar{E}(\mathbb{K})$. Subtracting gives $\mathcal{D}_L(\mathbb{K}) \le \mathcal{D}_L(\mathbb{K}^{+})$, which is the assertion.
--
--   **Formalization note.** `maximalRealSubfield K` is Mathlib's $\mathbb{K}^{+}$, and `IsCMField K` says that $\mathbb{K}$ is totally complex and quadratic over it; the `NumberField` and `IsTotallyReal` instances for $\mathbb{K}^{+}$ are the ones Mathlib supplies. Oddness of $p$ is the standing assumption of Section 1.1 of the source and is carried here for faithfulness to it; the argument just described does not use it, so the statement may later be strengthened by dropping `hp`.
-- source:
--   Preda Mihailescu, On CM Z_p-extensions and the Leopoldt conjecture for CM fields, https://arxiv.org/abs/1105.4544 (v4, 17 Feb 2016), Section 1.1 (Notations and fundamental facts), p. 4, verbatim: "For CM extensions K, the contraposition of the conjecture herewith reduces to the statement that K+ has more Z_p-extensions than just the cyclotomic one. It is this assumption which we shall use and lead to a contradiction. If Leopoldt fails thus for K+ and if L+/K+ is a further Z_p-extension, it will be totally real and L = L+ . K will be a CM extension." The passage rests on Iwasawa's theorem, recorded on the same page: "if Omega(K) is the maximal p-abelian p-ramified extension of K, then Gal(Omega(K)/K) ~ Z_p^n, where n = r_2 + 1 + D_L(K)" (K. Iwasawa, On Z_l-extensions of number fields, Ann. of Math. 98 (1973), 246-326). The standing hypothesis of Section 1.1 is "Let p be an odd rational prime" (p. 2).

import Definitions.Def_LeopoldtDefect

open NumberField

namespace Leopoldt
theorem defect_maximalRealSubfield_pos_of_defect_pos (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (hp : Odd p)
    (K : Type*) [Field K] [NumberField K] [IsCMField K] (h : 0 < defect p K) :
    0 < defect p (maximalRealSubfield K) := by sorry
end Leopoldt
